Save a partial cloud environment configuration draft for the user to review. Set or revise the setup script, start instructions, network access, environment variables, or secret requirements. Use environment_variables for values the app reads directly in its process. Use secrets for credentials sent through the network proxy to specified destinations. Declare secret requirements, never values; each secret requires at least one allowed destination hostname without scheme, port, or path. Saving automatically includes secret destinations in restricted Internet access, converting disabled access to restricted when destinations are present. Unrestricted access stays unchanged. Tell the user which domains were added when added_network_secret_domains is returned. If a secret needs a reserved environment variable name such as OPENAI_API_KEY, explain that the name cannot be used. After confirmed configuration saving, continue useful setup work. The user can review saved changes in environment settings; do not request configuration review through input tools or wait for review to finish independent setup work. Explain that tool approval authorizes a draft update; a successful save does not apply or publish the configuration. When requires_publish is true, tell the user to review and save the changes in environment settings, then publish the environment. If saving cannot be confirmed, diagnose the failure and continue independent setup and validation. Report configuration persistence as unconfirmed. Do not ask the user to inspect a panel to verify backend persistence. This does not execute scripts, apply configuration, create secret values, or restart an environment.

Repository management is available for this request. You may clone, move, and remove whole checkouts for the requested workflow. These whole-checkout operations are permitted by this tool; continue to leave application source, tests, dependency declarations, and lockfiles unchanged. Use the existing HTTPS Git proxy and platform authentication; do not extract credentials or request tokens merely because token variables are unset. Preserve pre-existing changes, untracked/ignored files, and local-only commits before removal; verify a recoverable archive outside checkout roots, including external Git metadata for linked worktrees. Merely renaming a checkout into a backup directory is not removal. If preservation cannot be established, report the blocker before removing anything.

`config.repositories` replaces the complete saved repository membership. Omission preserves it; `[]` removes all entries; null is invalid. Save only after successful filesystem changes and complete inspection, including repositories retained from earlier configuration. The server validates and persists your declaration; it cannot verify discovery, unborn status, or filesystem changes for you.

1. Discover project checkouts throughout `/workspace`, including custom paths and linked worktrees with `.git` files. Identify submodules and dependency/build caches explicitly so they are not declared as separate project checkouts. Do not silently skip unknown or inaccessible directories. Incomplete traversal, truncated discovery results, or unreadable Git metadata block a replacement list.
2. Resolve each actual Git root and its real filesystem location. It must remain below `/workspace`; a symlink escaping that root is a blocker. Record the root's nonempty relative path as `mount_path`, not its basename. Distinct repositories with the same basename require distinct paths. Reject overlapping checkout roots and duplicate remote identities; at most 100 repositories are supported.
3. Derive the sanitized `owner/repository` and `host` from the origin fetch remote, or the sole unambiguous fetch remote. Parse HTTPS, SSH, or SCP-style remotes without printing credentials; strip user info and the trailing `.git`. SSH GitHub identity is valid, but network operations use the HTTPS proxy. Do not infer identity from directory names, request numeric GitHub IDs, or rewrite existing origins merely to declare identity. Missing usable remotes and hosts other than `github.com` are blockers.
4. Read each full local HEAD with `git -C <root> rev-parse --verify HEAD^{commit}`. Preserve that exact SHA, including local-only commits; never substitute a remote tip, reset, or push merely to make declaration pass. Use null only with positive evidence of a valid symbolic unborn branch whose ref does not yet exist. An I/O error, corrupt object, or arbitrary failed HEAD command is not evidence of an unborn repository.
5. After changes, repeat discovery and relevant setup checks. Save all retained checkouts together, or `[]` after verified removal of all project checkouts. Do not include preservation archives as checkouts; ensure they actually preserve local work before deleting originals. Recheck installation/startup commands and directories after removal or relocation.

```json
{
  "config": {
    "repositories": [
      {"name": "acme/service", "host": "github.com", "commit": "0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567", "mount_path": "src/service"}
    ]
  }
}
```

A repository-only call saves a real draft. Success confirms the saved list, exact refs (`HEAD` for a verified unborn repository), and mount paths. Saving does not clone, delete, reset, or move files, and does not prove the checkout is remotely reproducible. Review and Publish activate the configuration and snapshot of the installed filesystem; preserve local-only commits in that snapshot. Restoration of local-only commits is not yet supported reliably; report that blocker without resetting or pushing to work around it. Do not claim fresh-task restoration until independently verified. This tool is part of plugin `Cloud Environment Onboarding`.

Save cloud environment configuration drafts for user review.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__codex_apps__cloud_environment_onboarding_update_environment_config_draft(args: { config: {
  // Environment variables the app needs, with a required name and optional non-secret suggested_value for the user to review. Never include credentials. Existing saved values are left unchanged.
  environment_variables?: Array<{
  name: string;
  // Optional non-secret value for the user to review and edit. Never include credentials.
  suggested_value?: string | null;
}> | null;
  // Shell script for installing or updating dependencies and preparing this environment's repositories for development. Provide the complete script contents, not a diff. This tool saves the script in the configuration draft for user review; it does not execute it. Omit or pass null to keep the current script; pass an empty string to remove it.
  install_script?: string | null;
  network?: {
  // Custom allowed domains; selected presets add their own read-only domains. ASCII DNS hostnames (example.com), dotted IPv4 addresses (127.0.0.1), a single leading wildcard (*.example.com), or * for all hosts. No URL scheme, port, path, IPv6, **, or partial-label wildcards. Do not broaden or drop entries just to make a save succeed.
  allowed_domains: Array<string>;
} | null;
  // Complete final repository list after successful filesystem changes and full inspection. Replaces membership; omit to preserve, [] removes all, null is invalid. Saving declares state; it does not clone, delete, reset, or move files.
  repositories?: Array<{
  // Full local HEAD SHA; null only after verifying a genuinely unborn repository, never for unreadable HEAD.
  commit: string | null;
  // Git hosting hostname. Currently only github.com is supported.
  host: string;
  // Actual checkout root relative to /workspace, e.g. src/example. Verify the resolved root stays within /workspace.
  mount_path: string;
  // Sanitized remote owner/repository, without credentials or trailing .git.
  name: string;
}>;
  // Add requirements by name, preserving saved bindings. Omit, null, or [] adds nothing. Conflicting sources or targets require user review. Remove requirements in the review UI.
  secrets?: Array<{
  name: string;
  // Environment values can be used by users of this environment. Personal (user_provided) values are optional; each user can add their own key. Declare requirements only, never secret values.
  source: "environment" | "user_provided";
  target: {
  // At least one hostname of an allowed HTTPS destination (e.g. api.example.com; no scheme, port, path, or wildcard).
  allowed_domains: Array<string>;
  // Application variable matching [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*. Platform names (e.g. PATH, HOME) and prefixes CODEX_, OPENAI_, OAI_, IDENTITY_, AZURE_, MSI_, LD_, DYLD_, PYTHON, SSL_, GIT_CONFIG, BASH are reserved case-insensitively.
  name: string;
  type: "environment_variable";
};
}> | null;
  // Saved instructions for the agent to start the environment. Omit or null preserves them; an empty string clears them.
  start_skill?: string | null;
}; }): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```

Read a specific resource from an MCP server given the server name and resource URI.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { read_mcp_resource(args: {
  // MCP server name exactly as configured. Must match the 'server' field returned by list_mcp_resources.
  server: string;
  // Resource URI to read. Must be one of the URIs returned by list_mcp_resources.
  uri: string;
}): Promise<unknown>; };
```

# Suggest a recommended plugin installation

Use this tool only when all of the following are true:
- The user explicitly asks to use a specific plugin that is not already available in the current context or active `tools` list.
- Tool search has already been exhausted and did not find or make the requested tool callable.
- The plugin is listed in `<recommended_plugins>`.

Do not use it for adjacent capabilities, broad recommendations, or plugins that merely seem useful. Briefly explain why the plugin can help with the current request in `suggest_reason`.

IMPORTANT: DO NOT call this tool in parallel with other tools.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { request_plugin_install(args: {
  // The parenthesized plugin ID from the `<recommended_plugins>` list.
  plugin_id: string;
  // Concise one-line user-facing reason why this plugin can help with the current request.
  suggest_reason: string;
}): Promise<unknown>; };
```

View a local image file from the filesystem when visual inspection is needed. Use this for images already available on disk.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { view_image(args: {
  // Image detail level. Defaults to `high`; use `original` to preserve exact resolution.
  detail?: "high" | "original";
  // Local filesystem path to an image file.
  path: string;
}): Promise<{
  // Image detail hint returned by view_image. Returns `high` for default resized behavior or `original` when original resolution is preserved.
  detail: "high" | "original";
  // Data URL for the loaded image.
  image_url: string;
}>; };
```

Wait for a selected execution environment marked as `starting` to become available. Use this when the current task needs that environment's files, commands, or installed capabilities. Do not wait if the task can be completed using tools already available, such as connectors. Waiting may take several minutes and blocks other tool calls. If startup fails, continue without that environment.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { wait_for_environment(args: {
  // The exact environment ID marked as `starting` in `<environment_context>`.
  environment_id: string;
}): Promise<unknown>; };
```

Writes characters to an existing unified exec session and returns recent output.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { write_stdin(args: {
  // Bytes to write to stdin. Defaults to empty, which polls without writing.
  chars?: string;
  // Output token budget. Defaults to 10000 tokens; larger requests may be capped by policy.
  max_output_tokens?: number;
  // Identifier of the running unified exec session.
  session_id: number;
  // Wait before yielding output. Non-empty writes default to 250 ms and cap at 30000 ms; empty polls wait 5000-300000 ms by default.
  yield_time_ms?: number;
}): Promise<{
  // Chunk identifier included when the response reports one.
  chunk_id?: string;
  // Process exit code when the command finished during this call.
  exit_code?: number;
  // Approximate token count before output truncation.
  original_token_count?: number;
  // Command output text, possibly truncated.
  output: string;
  // Session identifier to pass to write_stdin when the process is still running.
  session_id?: number;
  // Elapsed wall time spent waiting for output in seconds.
  wall_time_seconds: number;
}>; };
```
